.Em usage ]
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This command allows PKCS#12 files (sometimes referred to as PFX files)
to be created and parsed.
PKCS#12 files are used by several programs including Netscape, MSIE and
MS Outlook.
.Sh OPTIONS
There are a lot of options the meaning of some depends of whether a
PKCS#12 file is being created or parsed.
By default a PKCS#12 file is parsed.
A PKCS#12 file can be created by using the
.Sy -export
option (see below).
The PKCS#12 export encryption and MAC options such as
.Sy -certpbe
and
.Sy -iter
and many further options such as
.Sy -chain
are relevant only with
.Sy -export .
Conversely, the options regarding encryption of private keys when
outputting PKCS#12 input are relevant only when the
.Sy -export
option is not given.
.Pp
The default encryption algorithm is AES-256-CBC with PBKDF2 for key
derivation.
.Pp
When encountering problems loading legacy PKCS#12 files that involve,
for example, RC2-40-CBC, try using the
.Sy -legacy
option and, if needed, the
.Sy -provider-path
option.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -help
Print out a usage message.
.It Sy -passin Em arg
The password source for the input, and for encrypting any private keys
that are output.
For more information about the format of
.Sy arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -passout Em arg
The password source for output files.
.It Sy -password Em arg
With
.Sy -export ,
.Sy -password
is equivalent to
.Sy -passout ,
otherwise it is equivalent to
.Sy -passin .
.It Sy -twopass
Prompt for separate integrity and encryption passwords: most software
always assumes these are the same so this option will render such
PKCS#12 files unreadable.
Cannot be used in combination with the options
.Sy -password ,
.Sy -passin
if importing from PKCS#12, or
.Sy -passout
if exporting.
.It Sy -nokeys
No private keys will be output.
.It Sy -nocerts
No certificates will be output.
.It Sy -noout
This option inhibits all credentials output, and so the input is just
verified.
.It Sy -legacy
Use legacy mode of operation and automatically load the legacy provider.
If OpenSSL is not installed system-wide, it is necessary to also use,
for example,
.Qo Li -provider-path ./providers Qc
or to set the environment variable
.Sy OPENSSL_MODULES
to point to the directory where the providers can be found.
.Pp
In the legacy mode, the default algorithm for certificate encryption is
RC2_CBC or 3DES_CBC depending on whether the RC2 cipher is enabled in
the build.
The default algorithm for private key encryption is 3DES_CBC. If the
legacy option is not specified, then the legacy provider is not loaded
and the default encryption algorithm for both certificates and private
keys is AES_256_CBC with PBKDF2 for key derivation.
.It Sy -engine Em id
See
.Xr openssl 1 .
This option is deprecated.
.It Sy -provider Em name
.It Sy -provider-path Em path
.It Sy -provparam Em [name:]key=value
.It Sy -propquery Em propq
See
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr provider 7 ,
and
.Xr property 7 .
.It Sy -rand Em files Ns , Sy -writerand Em file
See
.Xr openssl 1
for details.
.El
.Ss PKCS#12 input (parsing) options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -in Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
This specifies the input filename or URI. Standard input is used by
default.
Without the
.Sy -export
option this must be PKCS#12 file to be parsed.
For use with the
.Sy -export
option see the
.Sx PKCS#12 output (export) options
section.
.It Sy -out Em filename
The filename to write certificates and private keys to, standard output
by default.
They are all written in PEM format.
.It Sy -info
Output additional information about the PKCS#12 file structure,
algorithms used and iteration counts.
.It Sy -nomacver
Don't attempt to verify the integrity MAC.
.It Sy -clcerts
Only output client certificates (not CA certificates).
.It Sy -cacerts
Only output CA certificates (not client certificates).
.It Sy -aes128 Ns , Sy -aes192 Ns , Sy -aes256
Use AES to encrypt private keys before outputting.
.It Sy -aria128 Ns , Sy -aria192 Ns , Sy -aria256
Use ARIA to encrypt private keys before outputting.
.It Sy -camellia128 Ns , Sy -camellia192 Ns , Sy -camellia256
Use Camellia to encrypt private keys before outputting.
.It Sy -des
Use DES to encrypt private keys before outputting.
.It Sy -des3
Use triple DES to encrypt private keys before outputting.
.It Sy -idea
Use IDEA to encrypt private keys before outputting.
.It Sy -noenc
Don't encrypt private keys at all.
.It Sy -nodes
This option is deprecated since OpenSSL 3.0; use
.Sy -noenc
instead.
.El
.Ss PKCS#12 output (export) options
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy -export
This option specifies that a PKCS#12 file will be created rather than
parsed.
.It Sy -out Em filename
This specifies filename to write the PKCS#12 file to.
Standard output is used by default.
.It Sy -in Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
This specifies the input filename or URI. Standard input is used by
default.
With the
.Sy -export
option this is a file with certificates and a key, or a URI that refers
to a key accessed via an engine.
The order of credentials in a file doesn't matter but one private key
and its corresponding certificate should be present.
If additional certificates are present they will also be included in the
PKCS#12 output file.
.It Sy -inkey Em filename Ns | Ns Em uri
The private key input for PKCS12 output.
If this option is not specified then the input file
.Pf ( Sy -in
argument) must contain a private key.
If no engine is used, the argument is taken as a file.
If the
.Sy -engine
option is used or the URI has prefix
.Qo Li org.openssl.engine: Qc
then the rest of the URI is taken as key identifier for the given
engine.
.It Sy -certfile Em filename
An input file with extra certificates to be added to the PKCS#12 output
if the
.Sy -export
option is given.
.It Sy -passcerts Em arg
The password source for certificate input such as
.Sy -certfile
and
.Sy -untrusted .
For more information about the format of
.Sy arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -chain
If this option is present then the certificate chain of the end entity
certificate is built and included in the PKCS#12 output file.
The end entity certificate is the first one read from the
.Sy -in
file if no key is given, else the first certificate matching the given
key.
The standard CA trust store is used for chain building, as well as any
untrusted CA certificates given with the
.Sy -untrusted
option.
.It Sy -untrusted Em filename
An input file of untrusted certificates that may be used for chain
building, which is relevant only when a PKCS#12 file is created with the
.Sy -export
option and the
.Sy -chain
option is given as well.
Any certificates that are actually part of the chain are added to the
output.
.It Sy -CAfile Em file Ns , Sy -no-CAfile Ns , Sy -CApath Em dir Ns , Sy -no-CApath Ns , Sy -CAstore Em uri Ns , Sy -no-CAstore
See
.Xr openssl-verification-options 1
for details.
.It Sy -name Em friendlyname
This specifies the "friendly name" for the certificates and private key.
This name is typically displayed in list boxes by software importing the
file.
.It Sy -caname Em friendlyname
This specifies the "friendly name" for other certificates.
This option may be used multiple times to specify names for all
certificates in the order they appear.
Netscape ignores friendly names on other certificates whereas MSIE
displays them.
.It Sy -CSP Em name
Write
.Em name
as a Microsoft CSP name.
The password source for the input, and for encrypting any private keys
that are output.
For more information about the format of
.Sy arg
see
.Xr openssl-passphrase-options 1 .
.It Sy -LMK
Add the "Local Key Set" identifier to the attributes.
.It Sy -keyex Ns | Ns Sy -keysig
Specifies that the private key is to be used for key exchange or just
signing.
This option is only interpreted by MSIE and similar MS software.
Normally "export grade" software will only allow 512 bit RSA keys to be
used for encryption purposes but arbitrary length keys for signing.
The
.Sy -keysig
option marks the key for signing only.
Signing only keys can be used for S/MIME signing, authenticode (ActiveX
control signing) and SSL client authentication, however, due to a bug
only MSIE 5.0 and later support the use of signing only keys for SSL
client authentication.
.It Sy -keypbe Em alg Ns , Sy -certpbe Em alg
These options allow the algorithm used to encrypt the private key and
certificates to be selected.
Any PKCS#5 v1.5 or PKCS#12 PBE algorithm name can be used (see
.Sx NOTES
section for more information).
If a cipher name (as output by
.Qo Li openssl list -cipher-algorithms Qc )
is specified then it is used with PKCS#5 v2.0. For interoperability
reasons it is advisable to only use PKCS#12 algorithms.
.Pp
Special value
.Qo Li NONE Qc
disables encryption of the private key and certificates.
.It Sy -descert
Encrypt the certificates using triple DES. By default the private key
and the certificates are encrypted using AES-256-CBC unless the
'-legacy' option is used.
If '-descert' is used with the '-legacy' then both, the private key and
the certificates are encrypted using triple DES.
.It Sy -macalg Em digest
Specify the MAC digest algorithm.
If not included SHA256 will be used.
.It Sy -pbmac1_pbkdf2
Use PBMAC1 with PBKDF2 for MAC protection of the PKCS#12 file.
.It Sy -pbmac1_pbkdf2_md Em digest
Specify the PBKDF2 KDF digest algorithm.
If not specified, SHA256 will be used.
Unless
.Qo Li -pbmac1_pbkdf2 Qc
is specified, this parameter is ignored.
.It Sy -iter Em count
This option specifies the iteration count for the encryption key and
MAC. The default value is 2048.
.Pp
To discourage attacks by using large dictionaries of common passwords
the algorithm that derives keys from passwords can have an iteration
count applied to it: this causes a certain part of the algorithm to be
repeated and slows it down.
The MAC is used to check the file integrity but since it will normally
have the same password as the keys and certificates it could also be
attacked.
.It Sy -noiter Ns , Sy -nomaciter
By default both encryption and MAC iteration counts are set to 2048,
using these options the MAC and encryption iteration counts can be set
to 1, since this reduces the file security you should not use these
options unless you really have to.
Most software supports both MAC and encryption iteration counts.
MSIE 4.0 doesn't support MAC iteration counts so it needs the
.Sy -nomaciter
option.
.It Sy -maciter
This option is included for compatibility with previous versions, it
used to be needed to use MAC iterations counts but they are now used by
default.
.It Sy -macsaltlen
This option specifies the salt length in bytes for the MAC. The salt
length should be at least 16 bytes as per NIST SP 800-132. The default
value is 8 bytes for backwards compatibility.
.It Sy -nomac
Do not attempt to provide the MAC integrity.
This can be useful with the FIPS provider as the PKCS12 MAC requires
PKCS12KDF which is not an approved FIPS algorithm and cannot be
supported by the FIPS provider.
.It Sy -jdktrust
Export pkcs12 file in a format compatible with Java keystore usage.
This option accepts a string parameter indicating the trust oid name to
be granted to the certificate it is associated with.
Currently only "anyExtendedKeyUsage" is defined.
Note that, as Java keystores do not accept PKCS12 files with both
trusted certificates and keypairs, use of this option implies the
setting of the
.Sy -nokeys
option
.El
.Sh NOTES
Although there are a large number of options most of them are very
rarely used.
For PKCS#12 file parsing only
.Sy -in
and
.Sy -out
need to be used for PKCS#12 file creation
.Sy -export
and
.Sy -name
are also used.
.Pp
If none of the
.Sy -clcerts ,
.Sy -cacerts
or
.Sy -nocerts
options are present then all certificates will be output in the order
they appear in the input PKCS#12 files.
There is no guarantee that the first certificate present is the one
corresponding to the private key.
Certain software which tries to get a private key and the corresponding
certificate might assume that the first certificate in the file is the
one corresponding to the private key, but that may not always be the
case.
Using the
.Sy -clcerts
option will solve this problem by only outputting the certificate
corresponding to the private key.
If the CA certificates are required then they can be output to a
separate file using the
.Sy -nokeys
.Sy -cacerts
options to just output CA certificates.
.Pp
The
.Sy -keypbe
and
.Sy -certpbe
algorithms allow the precise encryption algorithms for private keys and
certificates to be specified.
Normally the defaults are fine but occasionally software can't handle
triple DES encrypted private keys, then the option
.Sy -keypbe
.Em PBE-SHA1-RC2-40
can be used to reduce the private key encryption to 40 bit RC2. A
complete description of all algorithms is contained in
.Xr openssl-pkcs8 1 .
.Pp
Prior 1.1 release passwords containing non-ASCII characters were encoded
in non-compliant manner, which limited interoperability, in first hand
with Windows.
But switching to standard-compliant password encoding poses problem
accessing old data protected with broken encoding.
For this reason even legacy encodings is attempted when reading the
data.
If you use PKCS#12 files in production application you are advised to
convert the data, because implemented heuristic approach is not MT-safe,
its sole goal is to facilitate the data upgrade with this command.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Parse a PKCS#12 file and output it to a PEM file:
.Bd -literal
 openssl pkcs12 -in file.p12 -out file.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Output only client certificates to a file:
.Bd -literal
 openssl pkcs12 -in file.p12 -clcerts -out file.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Don't encrypt the private key:
.Bd -literal
 openssl pkcs12 -in file.p12 -out file.pem -noenc
.Ed
.Pp
Print some info about a PKCS#12 file:
.Bd -literal
 openssl pkcs12 -in file.p12 -info -noout
.Ed
.Pp
Print some info about a PKCS#12 file in legacy mode:
.Bd -literal
 openssl pkcs12 -in file.p12 -info -noout -legacy
.Ed
.Pp
Create a PKCS#12 file from a PEM file that may contain a key and
certificates:
.Bd -literal
 openssl pkcs12 -export -in file.pem -out file.p12 -name "My PSE"
.Ed
.Pp
Include some extra certificates:
.Bd -literal
 openssl pkcs12 -export -in file.pem -out file.p12 -name "My PSE" \e
  -certfile othercerts.pem
.Ed
.Pp
Export a PKCS#12 file with data from a certificate PEM file and from a
further PEM file containing a key, with default algorithms as in the
legacy provider:
.Bd -literal
 openssl pkcs12 -export -in cert.pem -inkey key.pem -out file.p12 -legacy
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr openssl-pkcs8 1 ,
.Xr ossl_store-file 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Sy -engine
option was deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0. The
.Sy -nodes
option was deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0, too; use
.Sy -noenc
instead.
